  "description": "<p>While inspecting the compiler warnings i stumbled over this in DOReaderCache.put(DOReader reader):</p>\n\n<div class=\"code panel\" style=\"border-width: 1px;\"><div class=\"codeContent panelContent\">\n<pre class=\"code-java\">        <span class=\"code-object\">String</span> pid = <span class=\"code-keyword\">null</span>;\n        <span class=\"code-keyword\">try</span> {\n            pid = reader.GetObjectPID();\n        } <span class=\"code-keyword\">catch</span> (Exception e) {\n        }\n        <span class=\"code-object\">Long</span> time = <span class=\"code-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"code-object\">Long</span>(<span class=\"code-object\">System</span>.currentTimeMillis());\n        List l = <span class=\"code-keyword\">new</span> ArrayList();\n        l.add(reader);\n        l.add(time);\n</pre>\n</div></div>\n\n<p>later this list gets added to a Map m_readers, with the PID being the Key.</p>\n\n<p>As you can see two different types (String, Long) get added to a List and type and semantics are dependent on the index in the list only. Although Lists are guaranteed to be ordered  i imho consider this an anti-pattern since  it makes lots of things more complex then they need to be.  (e.g. Collections.sort()).</p>",
